# Continue as a holder

The command, or an AtE naming these records, approves the records this skill
writes; other work needs AtE (rules file).

1. `<records>/bin/ptt resume NAME`, or without a name `<records>/bin/ptt
   resume`: it only lists the holders, those with a handover first, then by
   their last write, each with its named resume, and binds none, also when
   one holder alone left a handover. Show the list and ask which one; stop:
   only the user's named resume continues a holder. A holder
   marked "may still be working" wrote minutes ago from its own session:
   continuing it takes the role from that session, so say so.
2. If refused (NAME holds nothing, or this session already is another
   holder), report why and stop; never work around it.
3. NAME is your holder name from now on, as if you had taken it
   (`/ptt-take`, step 3). The tool binds it to this session where the agent
   tool gives a session ID. If the binding moved to a different session,
   the earlier session can no longer write as it: remind the user to stop
   using that earlier session. If already bound here, continue to read the
   handover without taking again; a session bound before session names
   existed gets its name now. Report every `note:` the tool printed,
   and pass on the printed line that renames this session, with its reason
   (`/ptt-take`, step 3).
4. Read what NAME starts from (`/ptt-take`, step 4) and its status record
   (`status.md`, or `<records>/handoff/integrator.md`), then report where it
   stands and the next step.
   Report as the rules file says: an overview line per item, then one topic
   at a time.
   Then go on read-only as after a take
   (`/ptt-take`, step 4); with work in progress, a task holder's plan covers
   what remains, from `status.md`.
   After acting on an amendment, use `<records>/bin/ptt adopt TASK N[,N]
   --as NAME` on the user's AtE; if you disagree, write a note to the designer.
   For the final report (plan or decision list), apply the rename check in
   `/ptt-take`, step 4: when required, start with `1. /rename <name>` using
   the recorded session name, then the business items.

In Claude Code, `/clear` starts a fresh session ID: use `/ptt-resume NAME`
to continue the holder and reload its handover.

In Codex invoke this skill as `$ptt-resume`. A fresh chat or fork must
resume to take over; continuing or compacting the same thread keeps its
binding. `$ptt-whoami` checks it without changing it.
